<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahagún</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sahagún is a town in the province of León in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the pilgrimage road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. Its Benedictine monastery of San Benito, richly endowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> and reformed on the model of Cluny, was one of the most powerful in medieval León, and Alfonso was buried there. In 1085 the king granted a charter, or fuero, to the merchants and settlers, many of them French, who formed the town around it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahagun.jpg" alt="Sahagún" width="320" height="140" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian González · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PanoSahag%C3%BAn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
